  "summary": "Purple-and-white shell belts that speak treaties, memory and law.",
  "description": "Wampum — cylindrical beads drilled from quahog clam and whelk shell in purple and white — became the diplomatic technology of the Haudenosaunee and coastal Algonquians: belts of thousands of beads whose patterned figures (ships, diamonds, parallel rows) encoded treaties, condolences and lineage. The Two Row Wampum (Guswenta) binds Dutch and then other nations to coexistence in parallel courses; the Hiawatha Belt encodes the five-nation confederacy itself. Runners read belts aloud at councils — the belt is the treaty's archive. Contemporary Haudenosaunee nations continue to make and repatriate belts, and wampum remains legal-metaphor in Canada's treaty discourse.",
  "culturalContext": "Wampum is Haudenosaunee diplomacy's material law: the Two Row and Hiawatha belts bind nations in living treaty memory, belts are read aloud at councils, and contemporary repatriation and commemoration (Guswenta 400) keep wampum central to Indigenous-Settler legal discourse in North America.",
